About the author
----------------

Maurizio Cammalleri is a system architect based in Italy. In more than
thirty years of work, Maurizio has designed embedded systems, industrial
control software and high-performance web platforms, and has led technical
teams as CTO and CIO.

Luxia is part of Prabanta (https://github.com/prabanta-dev), a family of
tools written in C with no dependencies beyond the C library:

  - Luxia, an extended Pascal with the rigour of Ada: the language, its
    front end and the program luxia (https://github.com/prabanta-dev/luxia);
  - Limba, what every language of the family shares: the intermediate
    representation, its optimiser and a reference interpreter
    (https://github.com/prabanta-dev/limba);
  - Meri, the register-based virtual machine that runs the IR, with a
    bytecode compiler, ahead-of-time and just-in-time compilation
    (https://github.com/prabanta-dev/meri).

The family also has Janas, tools for artificial intelligence, whose aim is
to lower the hardware that private AI needs
(https://github.com/prabanta-dev/janas), and Napa, for web
infrastructure, and Prama, for high-performance computing, both still
being defined.

Other public projects by the same author, in Free Pascal:

  - SedaiBasic2, a BASIC interpreter with a virtual machine, whose ideas
    Meri takes, not its code (https://github.com/camauri/SedaiBasic2);
  - Sedai Audio Foundation, an audio synthesis library with five engines:
    subtractive, FM, wavetable, additive and SID
    (https://github.com/camauri/SedaiAudio).

Writing: https://cammalleri.substack.com/


Others
------

luxia contains the code of others that Limba and Meri contain, and
nothing more: their AUTHORS name it. Meri computes the integers of any
size with mini-gmp, from GNU MP 6.3.0, contributed to the GNU project by
Niels Möller, with additional functionalities and improvements by Marco
Bodrato; Copyright (C) Free Software Foundation, Inc., under the GNU LGPL
version 3 or the GNU GPL version 2, or later.
